{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>You shall not bow down to them nor serve them.</b> In any manner of worship at all, and even if his intention is not to remove himself from the dominion of the Holy One, blessed be He — rather, all the worships, all of them, are for His name alone. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: 'You shall not bow down to them nor serve them' — even in a degrading manner, such as exposing oneself to Pe'or or throwing a stone to Merkulis — 'you shall not serve them,' whether this is its customary manner of worship or whether this is not its customary manner of worship. 'You shall not bow down to them nor serve them' — needless to say with intent, but even under coercion he is not permitted, and he must be killed rather than transgress. And therefore Hananiah, Mishael, and Azariah surrendered themselves, for if coercion were permitted they would not have delivered themselves to the fiery furnace. 'You shall not bow down to them nor serve them' — needless to say deliberately, but even unintentionally; and this is what we learned: If one's coins were scattered before an idol, he must not bend down and pick them up, because he appears as one bowing down to the idol; if a thorn was lying before an idol, he must not bend down and pick it up, because he appears as one bowing down to the idol — rather, he should sit on the ground and pick it up while seated. <b>For I, YHWH your Elohim,</b> — I alone; it is not fitting that you exchange me for another, and if you exchange me — 'I am El,' that is to say, there is power in My hand to punish you, and 'I am jealous' — He who leaves Me and worships another, I will visit this sin upon him to four generations, and this visitation is not for good, to cleanse the sins, like that of the Thirteen Attributes, but it is to destroy and annihilate everything, and therefore it says 'to those who hate Me.'" "<b>And know</b> that you will not find in all the Torah any jealousy on the part of the Holy One, blessed be He, except in the sin of idolatry. And the reason is that since Israel accepted upon themselves the divinity of the Holy One, blessed be He, in the receiving of the Torah, they are taken as His portion in singularity, and they are a people singled out for a singular Master; and when they slip away from Him and turn to other gods, it is fitting that He be jealous of them, as a man who is jealous of his wife when she goes to others. And from this you will not find in all the books of the Prophets that any prophet took any nation to task regarding the matter of idolatry, except Israel alone — as it is written (Deuteronomy 4:19–20): ״אשר חלק ה׳ אלהיך אותם לכל העמים תחת כל השמים״ — 'which YHWH your Elohim has allotted to all the peoples under all the heavens' — ״ואתכם לקח ה׳״ — 'but you has YHWH taken.'"], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:01:27.185882025Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}